<topic id="GUID-918C0DAB-CC1D-4858-A19F-F7AF12BEED40" xml:lang="en-US"> 
  <title>Resource Priority</title> 
  <body> 
    <section id="SECTION_586397F8CE63450C8094FC1AE430FD8F"> 
      <title>Overview</title> 
      <p> 
      <ul id="UL_FAEF600B37EA4FABB08DAE85216B85A7"> 
        <li id="LI_C3A6D282A6EB4E0A886D7F0951E717F4"> Simple way for OS to provide thread priority hints to SOC - enables SOC to allocate resources accordingly ​ . 
        </li> 
        <li id="LI_D5D01462E2784A9FA105D380C5E8453D"> Allows Resources to change and evolve without needing for OS updates. 
        </li> 
      </ul> 
      </p> 
    </section> 
    <section id="SECTION_13DD720136944B1AAC0DBBA5F8611DCA"> 
      <title> Configuration of Resource Priority is Model Specific</title> 
      <p> 
      <ul id="UL_B9C02FB6B08049768C23B57EAE7FFDB4"> 
        <li id="LI_5B1542A9D7A44290B2C382E2FEA050D8"> Leverage/extend existing RDT to support new Resource Priority Mode 
          <ul id="UL_7A4A818A37864EB5BC33F30363D3188E"> 
            <li id="LI_F687F8B5A9E744B9911D259265553ED3"> BIOS detects Resource Priority Feature through CPUID enumeration 
            </li> 
            <li id="LI_179DB1B71E02440CA3B02D95B054F22C"> BIOS enables RP and configures SOC resources (model specific) with Priority: CLOS Mapping, RPC Config(s) and ACPI Table that describes RP arch interface for OS / Driver 
            </li> 
            <li id="LI_0BF75EDA209A4C358E5F6EE5F3309B0F"> OS Detects Resource Priority Feature through CPUID enumeration and/or ACPI 
            </li> 
            <li id="LI_AA72E9C7524D49C2BC3D001F59CD286B"> OS Enables RP through architectural MSRs for core(s) and uncore 
            </li> 
            <li id="LI_5388C62A42B74C28B2F8F3B2BA96CA20"> OS maps QoS to CLOS 
            </li> 
            <li id="LI_9CED980B64DF4BA2A4764DB0F2FE37FA"> OS Specifies Per Thread Priority via PRQ_ASSOC CLOS MSR (similar to HWP) 
            </li> 
          </ul> 
        </li> 
      </ul> 
      </p>
